1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,500
Welcome to this new analysis. Today we dive into a truly extraordinary economic article,

2
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:11,660
published in the Econometrica journal, which maps intergenerational mobility in 27 African nations.

3
00:00:11,660 --> 00:00:15,500
And the scale of this research is simply astounding.

4
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:19,039
We're talking about census data from over 16 million individuals,

5
00:00:19,039 --> 00:00:23,140
used to reconstruct educational trajectories starting from the 1960s.

6
00:00:23,140 --> 00:00:28,240
It's a rigorous exploration that goes beyond the usual simplistic narratives about the African continent,

7
00:00:28,240 --> 00:00:32,579
based solely and exclusively on numbers, and the results are surprising.

8
00:00:32,579 --> 00:00:36,840
So, is Africa really moving, and what truly drives educational success?

9
00:00:36,840 --> 00:00:38,740
Well, let's find out together.

10
00:00:38,740 --> 00:00:41,620
Here's our action plan for today.

11
00:00:41,620 --> 00:00:44,280
We'll understand exactly what mobility is.

12
00:00:44,280 --> 00:00:46,920
We'll take a look at the map of opportunity.

13
00:00:46,920 --> 00:00:50,620
Then we'll explore the deep divide between city and countryside.

14
00:00:50,620 --> 00:00:53,120
We'll investigate the historical roots of all this,

15
00:00:53,120 --> 00:00:56,120
and solve the dilemma between environment and selection.

16
00:00:56,160 --> 01:00:660
Finally, we'll understand why fate, fortunately, is not sealed at all.

17
01:00:660 --> 01:01:860
Let's begin.

18
01:01:860 --> 01:05:960
Point number one: what is intergenerational mobility?

19
01:05:960 --> 01:12:040
In short, what does it really mean to take a step forward compared to the previous generation?

20
01:12:040 --> 01:16:500
Before we can measure progress, we need a clear and objective metric.

21
01:16:500 --> 01:22:040
The central concept of this study is absolute upward intergenerational mobility.

22
01:22:040 --> 01:24:780
It sounds complicated, but it's actually very intuitive.

23
01:24:859 --> 01:28:340
In econometrics, measuring income in many developing countries

24
01:28:340 --> 01:30:960
is a real nightmare because data is scarce.

25
01:30:960 --> 01:34:299
But education, that is excellently documented.

26
01:34:299 --> 01:37:960
In this African context, the real watershed for a child's future

27
01:37:960 --> 01:40:040
is the completion of primary school.

28
01:40:040 --> 01:43:079
So, our key question is simple yet powerful.

29
01:43:079 --> 01:48:280
What is the probability that a child, born to parents who cannot read or did not finish primary school,

30
01:48:280 --> 01:50:439
will instead manage to obtain that diploma?

31
01:50:519 --> 01:56:299
And to answer this question, researchers did not take two or three small random samples;

32
01:56:299 --> 02:02:200
they analyzed data from 16 million people, 16 million,

33
02:02:200 --> 02:06:479
and all this distributed across 2846 different regions,

34
02:06:479 --> 02:11:199
an insane level of detail that reveals not only differences between states,

35
02:11:199 --> 02:13:319
but also those hidden within them.

36
02:13:319 --> 02:18:060
And this brings us to section two: mapping opportunities in Africa.

37
02:18:140 --> 02:21:740
A continent that reveals itself to be a true puzzle of contrasts.

38
02:21:740 --> 02:26:819
Observing how these educational opportunities are distributed across the territory,

39
02:26:819 --> 02:31:020
very clear patches of high mobility immediately stand out,

40
02:31:020 --> 02:33:819
and very dark zones of deep immobility.

41
02:33:819 --> 02:38:620
As you can see, the geography of opportunity is not at all uniform.

42
02:38:620 --> 02:42:620
There are nations that literally shine with their prospects,

43
02:42:620 --> 02:47:699
while others seem stuck in a vicious circle from generation to generation.

44
02:47:740 --> 02:49:540
Let's put the numbers in perspective.

45
02:49:540 --> 02:51:620
In countries like South Africa and Botswana,

46
02:51:620 --> 02:54:500
the probability that the child of uneducated parents

47
02:54:500 --> 02:59:300
will finish primary school exceeds 70%, which is a very high rate.

48
02:59:300 --> 03:03:060
On the other hand, in the Sahel or in countries like Sudan and Ethiopia,

49
03:03:060 --> 03:06:700
this same probability drops below 20%.

50
03:06:700 --> 03:08:340
This means that in those regions,

51
03:08:340 --> 03:10:540
being born into an uneducated family,

52
03:10:540 --> 03:15:460
there is more than an 80% chance of remaining uneducated oneself.

53
03:15:460 --> 03:17:340
It's a huge gap.

54
03:17:460 --> 03:19:500
But the true insight of this study

55
03:19:500 --> 03:23:340
is that it shows us how the real divide is often not between nations,

56
03:23:340 --> 03:25:020
but within them.

57
03:25:020 --> 03:26:500
Take the case of Ghana.

58
03:26:500 --> 03:27:699
There's a clear contrast.

59
03:27:699 --> 03:30:259
In the south of the country, opportunities are incredible.

60
03:30:259 --> 03:31:699
There's a lot of mobility.

61
03:31:699 --> 03:34:420
But as soon as we move towards the northern districts,

62
03:34:420 --> 03:37:020
the prospects drastically darken.

63
03:37:020 --> 03:38:340
The nation is the same,

64
03:38:340 --> 03:42:500
but a person's chances depend almost entirely on the district where they are born.

65
03:42:500 --> 03:43:900
Let's move on to point three.

66
03:43:900 --> 03:46:580
The gap between urban and rural areas.

67
03:46:660 --> 03:47:860
Because apparently,

68
03:47:860 --> 03:48:860
the place where you live

69
03:48:860 --> 03:50:860
makes all the difference in the world.

70
03:50:860 --> 03:52:780
Continuing to dig into the data,

71
03:52:780 --> 03:56:660
another invisible wall emerges, dividing the continent.

72
03:56:660 --> 03:59:660
Living in an urban center provides a huge boost.

73
03:59:660 --> 04:01:580
On average, residing in an urban area

74
04:01:580 --> 04:03:380
guarantees upward mobility

75
04:03:380 --> 04:06:900
18% higher than in rural areas.

76
04:06:900 --> 04:10:140
In African rural areas, there's a strong force of inertia.

77
04:10:140 --> 04:13:340
Surpassing one's parents' educational level is much rarer,

78
04:13:340 --> 04:16:460
and often educational opportunity is completely absent.

79
04:16:620 --> 04:19:259
Of course, this gap is not uniform everywhere.

80
04:19:259 --> 04:21:819
In poorer countries, it's a real chasm.

81
04:21:819 --> 04:27:259
In Ethiopia and Burkina Faso, there is a difference of as much as 40 percentage points between city and countryside.

82
04:27:259 --> 04:28:459
40.

83
04:28:459 --> 04:32:259
Conversely, in South Africa, it reduces to less than 10 percentage points.

84
04:32:259 --> 04:34:419
And there's another super interesting fact.

85
04:34:419 --> 04:36:339
On average, at the continental level,

86
04:36:339 --> 04:39:979
the mobility differences between boys and girls are minimal.

87
04:39:979 --> 04:43:819
The only significant exception is in the Sahel region in North Africa,

88
04:43:819 --> 04:46:779
where a strong initial disadvantage for girls persists.

89
04:46:779 --> 04:50:819
We reach section 4: where do these differences come from,

90
04:50:819 --> 04:54:099
the roots of development, between history and geography.

91
04:54:099 --> 04:56:819
Now, the crucial question.

92
04:56:819 --> 05:01:379
Why do some regions offer the world and others almost nothing?

93
05:01:379 --> 05:05:100
The data points directly to geography and history.

94
05:05:100 --> 05:10:899
Regions with the highest mobility today are generally close to coasts or capitals.

95
05:10:940 --> 05:14:700
Incredibly, even mountainous or rugged areas fare better

96
05:14:700 --> 05:18:100
because historically they offered shelter from shocks and conflicts.

97
05:18:100 --> 05:19:740
Then there's the environment.

98
05:19:740 --> 05:23:580
Less malaria means better health and therefore more development.

99
05:23:580 --> 05:25:780
And finally, the historical imprint.

100
05:25:780 --> 05:29:660
Districts crossed by colonial railways or those that hosted

101
05:29:660 --> 05:32:220
Christian missions and their schools

102
05:32:220 --> 05:35:700
show incredibly higher mobility rates.

103
05:35:700 --> 05:38:940
Even today, decades after independence.

104
05:38:980 --> 05:41:779
Do you know what the common thread in all this is?

105
05:41:779 --> 05:44:019
Initial literacy.

106
05:44:019 --> 05:49:219
All these historical factors determined who learned to read generations ago.

107
05:49:219 --> 05:52:620
The inertia mentioned in the document is almost unstoppable.

108
05:52:620 --> 05:56:300
Differences in the education rates of the older generation

109
05:56:300 --> 06:01:259
alone explain about 50% of the spatial disparities we observe today.

110
06:01:259 --> 06:05:779
The past continues to cast a giant, persistent shadow over the present.

111
06:05:820 --> 06:09:780
Section 5: effect of environment or selection.

112
06:09:780 --> 06:13:140
Let's try to untangle this causal puzzle.

113
06:13:140 --> 06:16:300
This brings us to the real economic core of the issue.

114
06:16:300 --> 06:19:420
A classic chicken-and-egg dilemma.

115
06:19:420 --> 06:22:900
We've just seen that certain regions offer high mobility.

116
06:22:900 --> 06:27:300
But is it really the region itself, with its infrastructure, that makes children flourish?

117
06:27:300 --> 06:31:940
Or do those areas seem virtuous only because they are full of highly motivated families

118
06:31:980 --> 06:36:939
who value education greatly and have simply decided to move there?

119
06:36:939 --> 06:40:300
It's a real tug-of-war between two forces.

120
06:40:300 --> 06:42:980
On one side, we have the selection effect.

121
06:42:980 --> 06:47:899
Parents inclined to invest in their children actively move to the best districts.

122
06:47:899 --> 06:50:819
On the other, the exposure effect.

123
06:50:819 --> 06:56:259
The idea that a place itself has the power to change a child's life outcome

124
06:56:259 --> 06:59:300
regardless of their family background.

125
06:59:340 --> 07:04:340
But how did researchers mathematically separate these two dynamics?

126
07:04:340 --> 07:06:379
The solution they found is brilliant.

127
07:06:379 --> 07:11:180
Researchers delved into internal migrant data, isolating children whose families

128
07:11:180 --> 07:13:020
moved to new regions.

129
07:13:020 --> 07:16:379
By tracing the difference in quality between destination and origin,

130
07:16:379 --> 07:21:540
they were able to measure the educational outcomes of children based on the exact age at which they moved.

131
07:21:540 --> 07:22:939
Let's put it this way.

132
07:22:939 --> 07:27:660
If a child arrives at two years old in a top region, their path will be different from that of their sibling

133
07:27:660 --> 07:31:580
who arrives at 14, when primary school is already a thing of the past.

134
07:31:580 --> 07:35:980
And by comparing the education of siblings within the same family,

135
07:35:980 --> 07:39:940
the researchers isolated the answer with surgical precision.

136
07:39:940 --> 07:44:860
The verdict is that both dynamics are real and operate simultaneously.

137
07:44:860 --> 07:50:460
The numbers speak for themselves: selection is massive and explains about two-thirds of the total differences.

138
07:50:460 --> 07:53:379
In short, those who seek opportunities move.

139
07:53:379 --> 07:57:580
But that exposure effect to the environment is undeniable and is the key.

140
07:57:699 --> 08:01:579
Every single extra year a child spends in a high-mobility district,

141
08:01:579 --> 08:07:180
before turning twelve, tangibly increases their chances of completing primary school.

142
08:07:180 --> 08:12:659
The data tells us that the critical time window for changing a life is from 5 to 11 years old.

143
08:12:659 --> 08:17:939
The environment in which those years are spent has a direct and causal impact on future success.

144
08:17:939 --> 08:20:060
And we come to the end of our journey.

145
08:20:060 --> 08:24:299
Point 6: Fate is not sealed. Let's look to the future.

146
08:24:420 --> 08:29:139
Recapitulating, the analysis of Pinge is a grandiose but logical picture.

147
08:29:139 --> 08:36:460
Africa's geography and centuries-old history planted the seeds of great initial disparities in literacy.

148
08:36:460 --> 08:42:340
These seeds germinated into a strong inertia, which today is reinforced by two drivers:

149
08:42:340 --> 08:46:299
motivated families moving to better areas

150
08:46:299 --> 08:52:340
and the real, tangible, and transformative impact that those regions have on the children who grow up there.

151
08:52:460 --> 08:57:700
And it is precisely this empirical evidence that leaves us with a powerful final thought.

152
08:57:700 --> 09:01:620
If we know for certain that growing up in a different environment during childhood

153
09:01:620 --> 09:04:860
literally alters a person's educational destiny,

154
09:04:860 --> 09:10:100
how should development policies rethink the fight against spatial inequality?

155
09:10:100 --> 09:14:460
How can we ensure that all of Africa, and not just a few fortunate districts,

156
09:14:460 --> 09:17:139
truly moves upward?

157
09:17:139 --> 09:19:220
This is the crucial question

158
09:19:220 --> 09:21:980
that these 16 million data points pose to us today,

159
09:21:980 --> 09:24:980
and it is a question we can no longer avoid.

160
09:24:980 --> 09:27:980
Thank you for joining us, see you at the next analysis.